WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.600
Cement casting, air-dry clay and found-object art, done safely.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.720
This is the video for module three of Advanced Home Décor and Upcycling.

3
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:20.020
Cement, air-dry clay and found objects let you make solid décor: planters, candle holders, trinket dishes and framed wall pieces.

4
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:29.550
This module teaches correct mixes, moulds from waste containers, safe handling of cement, and how to compose objects into art.

5
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:44.540
Wet cement is strongly alkaline. It can burn skin, and the dust irritates eyes and lungs. Wear waterproof gloves, goggles, a dust mask for dry powder, and long sleeves. Keep children and pets away.

6
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:56.840
If wet cement gets on skin, wash it off promptly with plenty of clean water. If it gets in the eyes, rinse with clean water for at least 15 minutes, and seek medical help.

7
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:06.380
Never rinse cement down drains. It sets underwater and blocks them. And never mix it in food containers you will use for food again.

8
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:16.940
Mix about 1 part cement to 2 parts fine, sieved sand. Add water little by little until it looks like thick porridge. Too wet, and it cracks.

9
00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:27.470
Use two nesting containers, like ice cream buckets. Oil every surface that touches the mix, and push an oiled cork into the base for a drainage hole.

10
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:35.950
Pour a base about 2 cm deep, press in the inner mould, and keep walls at least 1.5 to 2 cm thick.

11
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:46.220
Tap the sides for a minute or two to release air, weigh the inner mould down with stones, cover, and demould gently after about 24 hours.

12
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:58.440
For example, Esther in Abuja uses outer moulds of about 1 litre and inner moulds of about 0.5 litre. Each planter needs 0.5 litre of mix.

13
00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:08.050
Four need 2 litres. Wet mix shrinks compared to dry ingredients, so she prepares about 2.5 litres of dry material.

14
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:19.090
At 1 to 2, that is about 0.8 litre of cement and 1.7 litres of sand. She writes down the water too, so she can repeat the batch.

15
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:30.350
She keeps the planters wrapped in damp cloth inside nylon for 7 days. Cement left to dry in sun or harmattan wind becomes weak and dusty.

16
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:38.830
Air-dry clay hardens at room temperature, so you need no kiln. It suits trinket dishes, plaques and small vases.

17
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:58.380
Cold porcelain is 1 cup cornstarch, 1 cup PVA glue, 1 tablespoon of oil and 1 tablespoon of lemon juice or vinegar, stirred over low heat. Use a pot kept only for crafts afterwards, let the dough cool before handling, and keep children away from the stove.

18
00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:10.980
For example, Rahila in Makurdi rolls a slab 5 mm thick between two wooden strips, presses an old lace doily into it, and cuts six circles with a 10 cm cup.

19
00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:18.820
She drapes each in a lined saucer and dries them slowly under a loose cloth, turning them after the first day.

20
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:32.230
She seals each dish with two coats of varnish. Her label says: for rings and dry items, not for food or water. Even sealed, air-dry clay is never waterproof or food-safe.

21
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:48.670
Assemblage is art made by fixing found objects onto a surface. Done badly, it looks like glued rubbish. So lay everything out dry, find one focal point, repeat a colour, leave some empty space, and photograph each layout.

22
00:03:49.920 --> 00:04:03.850
For example, Joy in Warri has caps about 3 cm across. Ring 1, 12 cm from the centre, is about 75 cm round, so 25 caps. Three rings take 96 caps in total.

23
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:13.360
She fixes each cap with two-part epoxy, one ring at a time, and hangs the mirror with D-rings and picture wire screwed to the back.

24
00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:24.340
Use solvent glues outdoors, away from stoves and candles. Rest a hot glue gun on its stand, and keep a bowl of cold water nearby for burns.

25
00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:39.880
To remember. Protect your skin, eyes and lungs from cement, and keep casts damp for a week. Clay pieces are for dry items only. Lay out assemblages before gluing, and hang heavy pieces with D-rings and wire.

26
00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:50.750
Now cast and cure one planter with a curing log, make a clay sample set with a care label, and build a mini assemblage from at least 20 found objects.

27
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.040
Continue with the lessons below.
